WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.388
Change-oriented behavior: make the team better.

2
00:00:04.438 --> 00:00:11.121
In Yukl’s taxonomy this is category three of four, behaviors nine to twelve of fifteen,

3
00:00:11.171 --> 00:00:17.391
and its objective is to increase innovation, collective learning, and adaptation.

4
00:00:17.441 --> 00:00:24.279
It is how the Chancellor’s charge of speed, agility, and scale happens at the team level,

5
00:00:24.329 --> 00:00:30.471
one manager at a time. The detail most people miss: this is not reorganizations.

6
00:00:30.521 --> 00:00:38.366
Most of it is small and weekly. Behavior nine, advocating change: a new system is coming from central;

7
00:00:38.416 --> 00:00:46.105
explain the reason in your own words in Thursday’s team meeting, before Tuesday’s campus-wide email,

8
00:00:46.155 --> 00:00:53.458
and write down the objections to take back. Behavior ten, envisioning change: in two sentences,

9
00:00:53.508 --> 00:00:58.567
what your team will be able to do next year that it cannot do now.

10
00:00:58.617 --> 00:01:06.152
Behavior eleven, encouraging innovation: someone suggests a new way to run the weekly report; say,

11
00:01:06.202 --> 00:01:08.706
try it for two weeks and show me.

12
00:01:08.756 --> 00:01:16.910
Behavior twelve, facilitating collective learning: ten minutes after the event, what worked, what did not,

13
00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.244
what changes, written where the next person can find it.

14
00:01:21.294 --> 00:01:26.817
Watch for the difference. Forwarding the announcement is not advocating.

15
00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:31.461
That is not how we do it here is the opposite of innovation.

16
00:01:31.511 --> 00:01:38.814
Now flip the cards, then turn the page; the new system is coming and the team already hates it.

